  "Question": "A chord which is the perpendicular bisector of a radius of length \\(12\\) in a circle has length",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(3\\sqrt3\\)",
    "B": "\\(27\\)",
    "C": "\\(6\\sqrt3\\)",
    "D": "\\(12\\sqrt3\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Let \\(O\\) be the center, \\(P\\) the endpoint of the given radius, and \\(AB\\) the chord. Let \\(M\\) be their intersection. Since the chord perpendicularly bisects \\(OP\\),\n\\[\nOM=\\frac{12}{2}=6,\\qquad OM\\perp AB.\n\\]\nA line through a circle's center perpendicular to a chord bisects that chord, so \\(AM=MB\\). This reduces the chord-length problem to finding one leg of right triangle \\(OMA\\).\n\nThe hypotenuse \\(OA\\) is a radius of length \\(12\\). By the Pythagorean theorem,\n\\[\nAM=\\sqrt{12^2-6^2}\n=\\sqrt{108}=6\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nAB=2AM=\\boxed{12\\sqrt3},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
